{
  "Version": 2,
  "Name": "Baumeister1969-Filter1-oneM",
  "Source": "P. W. Baumeister, \"Radiant power flow and absorptance in thin films,\" Appl. Opt. 8, 423-436 (1969). doi:10.1364/AO.8.000423 - Table VI, p. 430 (Filter 1 = air (BL)^7 B 1.77L M stack 1; Stack 1 = 0.74 B(LB)^3 L quartz); Figs. 11, 14, 15",
  "Notes": "One-M (induced transmission) UV bandpass filter with a single 250 A Al film at 2536 A, matching stack 1 (ThOF2/cryolite quarter-wave stack)\nSpecification: Peak transmittance T_max at lambda0 = 2536 A (R = 0 at lambda0), matching-stack admittance ~ Y_max = 0.96 + j3.35.\nReported: p. 431-432: T_max of a 250 A Al film (n = 0.20, k = 2.80 at 2536 A) is 0.739, attained when the matching-stack admittance is Y_max = 0.96 + j3.35; all three Table VI filters have very nearly this peak transmittance with zero reflectance at lambda0 (Figs. 15-17). Fig. 15 (abscissa lambda0/lambda 0.7-1.3): peak T ~0.73 at lambda0/lambda = 1.0 with R ~0; narrowest passband of the three (Fig. 14, optical density vs lambda0/lambda). WARNING: as printed this design does NOT reproduce the paper: computed here T = 0.501, R = 0.178 at 253.6 nm, peak T = 0.609 at 255.3 nm, and stack 1 alone (0.74B (LB)^3 L on quartz) has admittance 2.10 + j1.78 instead of ~0.96 + j3.35. A misprint in Table VI (stack 1 or filter 1) is likely.\nNotes: Normal incidence assumed (no angle given). The notation is read left to right from the incident medium (air) to the substrate (quartz, n = 1.47), so layer 1 is next to air. All dielectric layers are quarter-wave optical thickness (QWOT) at lambda0 = 2536 A = 253.6 nm with constant indices; the Al film is a physical thickness with the nondispersive n - jk = 0.20 - j2.80 at 2536 A (Macleod sign convention; k = +2.80 here). B = thorium oxyfluoride n = 1.55, L = cryolite n = 1.36 (Table VI footnote; the text on p. 431 says cryolite 'index 1.35' for design 1 - the table value is used, and 1.35 does not fix the mismatch, nor does reversing stack 1). 0.74B = 0.74 QWOT of B, 1.77L = 1.77 QWOT of L. Expanded: (BL)^7 = 14 layers, B, 1.77L, M, then stack 1 = 0.74B, (LB)^3, L: 25 layers. Band 195-362 nm corresponds to lambda0/lambda = 1.3-0.7.\nLayers from Table VI, p. 430 (Filter 1 = air (BL)^7 B 1.77L M stack 1; Stack 1 = 0.74 B(LB)^3 L quartz); Figs. 11, 14, 15; layer 1 faces the incident medium.\nChecked with ThinFilmDesigner (2026-10-21), differs from the paper: ours T 0.501, R 0.178 at 253.6 nm; paper T 0.739 with R = 0 at 253.6 nm. Table VI as printed does not give the paper’s own T 0.739 with R 0; a misprinted thickness is the likely cause.",
